[2] Spearman(p_null_any, l_min) = -0.970  vs  Spearman(p_null_any, tau_C) = -0.216
    -> null-region production is governed by l_min, not by tau_C. There is no
       'lenient tau_C corner': p_null_any at l_min=1 falls only from 0.76 to 0.40
       across the entire tau_C range, and never reaches any candidate tolerance.

[3] natural-break check on the sorted p_null_any values:
    range 0.025-0.755 ; largest gap = 0.045 between 0.710 and 0.755
    second largest gap = 0.035 ; median gap = 0.0000
    -> NO distinct break: p_null_any is a smooth continuum, so ANY tolerance is arbitrary

[4] finite-B uncertainty (B = 200; Clopper-Pearson):
    eps = 0.05 : point rule keeps  42 cells, upper-bound rule keeps   0;
                42 cells (100% of those kept) are statistically indistinguishable
               from failing the rule at B = 200.
    eps = 0.10 : point rule keeps  86 cells, upper-bound rule keeps  61;
                25 cells (29% of those kept) are statistically indistinguishable
               from failing the rule at B = 200.
    eps = 0.20 : point rule keeps 116 cells, upper-bound rule keeps 101;
                15 cells (13% of those kept) are statistically indistinguishable
               from failing the rule at B = 200.
    typical CI width at the boundary: 0.086
    -> at B = 200 a cell with k = 20/200 has CI [0.062, 0.150]; distinguishing
       eps = 0.05 from eps = 0.10 is at the edge of this resolution.

